"""Original defensive architecture decisions, not vendor exam items."""
ITEMS=[
('Network segmentation already isolates the workload zones, but a gateway authorizes entry without checking access to each sensitive application inside a zone. The requirement is an explicit authorization check for each requested application. Which change directly supplies that missing check?',
 ['A policy enforcement point per application','Split each zone into smaller VLANs with stricter ACLs','Require device compliance before entry to the gateway','Add multifactor authentication at the zone gateway'],
 'Placing enforcement in front of each application makes every request carry its own authorization decision. Smaller VLANs, device checks or stronger login at the gateway still let a request inherit access to everything inside the zone.'),
('Access rules already evaluate each requested resource, but they rely on a device compliance result collected months earlier. Which change fixes the stale input to those decisions?',
 ['Pull a fresh MDM health signal for every evaluation','Add a second enforcement point in front of the resource','Shorten the user password expiry to thirty days','Require a hardware security key at every login'],
 'Dynamic decisions need fresh device posture, so the compliance signal must be current when each decision is made. More enforcement points or stronger user authentication would still rely on months-old device evidence.'),
('Two service tiers have correct user authentication but unrestricted east-west communication. A compromised reporting workload can reach a payment database it never needs. Which change directly limits this path?',
 ['Microsegment the tiers and allow only required flows','Require step-up MFA for reporting dashboard users','Encrypt the payment database at rest with a managed key','Send the reporting workload logs to the SIEM'],
 'Microsegmentation that permits only required service-to-service flows removes the reporting tier\'s path to the database. User MFA, encryption at rest and logging do not stop a compromised workload from connecting.'),
('A session remains authorized after the identity risk score rises sharply. New sessions are correctly blocked under the same policy. What capability is missing for existing access?',
 ['Continuous evaluation of open sessions','Longer token lifetimes to reduce reauthentication prompts','A stricter policy rule applied at initial login only','A daily report of users with elevated risk scores'],
 'Continuous evaluation lets the policy revoke or restrict a session already in progress when risk changes. Checks only at login, longer tokens or daily reports leave the existing session trusted.'),
('In a zero-trust design, a component combines identity, device condition and resource rules to decide whether a request should be allowed. Which architectural role does it perform?',
 ['Policy decision point','Policy enforcement point','Identity provider','Continuous diagnostics feed'],
 'The policy decision point evaluates the inputs and reaches the allow or deny decision. The enforcement point acts on that decision, and the identity provider and diagnostics feed only supply inputs.'),
('A gateway opens or closes a connection according to an authorization decision supplied by another component. Which role is the gateway performing?',
 ['Policy enforcement point','Policy administrator','Policy decision engine','Security information and event management'],
 'The enforcement point controls the actual connection based on a decision made elsewhere. The policy engine and administrator decide and configure; a SIEM only collects events.'),
('A service supplies a signed assertion about the authenticated user to an authorization system. It does not determine whether that user may read the requested file. Which role best fits?',
 ['An identity provider for the organization','Policy engine making the file access decision','Enforcement gateway in front of the file share','Data loss prevention service inspecting files'],
 'An identity provider supplies signed evidence of who the user is. Deciding file access belongs to the policy engine, and the gateway and DLP service act on traffic and content.'),
('An endpoint service reports patch state and integrity measurements to an access engine. It neither authenticates the human user nor opens the connection. Which role is this?',
 ['A device posture data source','A policy enforcement point on the endpoint','An identity provider for device users','A policy administrator for session setup'],
 'Reporting patch state and integrity measurements makes it a posture data source that the policy engine consumes. It does not authenticate users, decide access or open connections.'),
('A research platform must combine project membership, record sensitivity, device compliance and current location for each access decision. Which authorization model most directly expresses these independent conditions?',
 ['Attribute-based access control','Role-based access control','Discretionary access control','Mandatory access control'],
 'ABAC evaluates subject, resource and environment attributes together, which covers membership, sensitivity, device and location. Roles alone cannot express changing context.'),
('A warehouse application gives each employee one of a small set of stable job functions, with permissions managed for those functions. Which model most directly matches this design?',
 ['Role-based access control','Attribute-based access control','Rule-based access control','Discretionary access control'],
 'Permissions attached to a small set of job functions is RBAC. ABAC or rule-based models add conditions this simple design does not need.'),
('A collaboration system allows each document owner to grant or remove other users\' access as the owner alone chooses. Which model describes that authority?',
 ['Discretionary access control','Mandatory access control','Role-based access control','Risk-adaptive access control'],
 'Owner-granted access is discretionary access control. Mandatory access control uses central labels that owners cannot override.'),
('A system uses centrally assigned sensitivity labels and user clearances. File owners cannot override the resulting restrictions. Which model fits?',
 ['Mandatory access control','Discretionary access control','Role-based access control','Rule-based access control'],
 'Central labels and clearances that owners cannot override define mandatory access control. Discretionary control would let the owner decide.'),
('A team wants a pre-build check for unsafe code paths in its own source, before a runnable service exists. Which assurance approach fits this stage?',
 ['Static application security testing','Dynamic application security testing','Interactive application security testing','Runtime application self-protection'],
 'SAST analyzes source without running it, so it works before a service exists. DAST, IAST and RASP all need a running application.'),
('A tester has an authorized staging URL but no source or instrumentation. The goal is to assess externally observable application behavior. Which approach fits?',
 ['Dynamic application security testing','Static application security testing','Software composition analysis','Interactive application security testing'],
 'DAST exercises a running application from outside, which matches a URL with no source or agent. SAST and SCA need code or manifests, and IAST needs instrumentation.'),
('A release contains several open-source libraries. The immediate question is whether the selected dependency versions have known vulnerabilities. Which approach targets that question?',
 ['Software composition analysis','Static analysis of first-party code','Dynamic scanning of the staging site','Manual peer review of pull requests'],
 'SCA identifies third-party components and versions and matches them to known vulnerabilities. Analyzing first-party code or the running site does not establish dependency status.'),
('A test environment can collect application internals while realistic requests execute. The team wants to link observed requests to the code paths handling them. Which approach fits?',
 ['Interactive application security testing','Dynamic application security testing','Runtime application self-protection','Fuzz testing of the API inputs'],
 'IAST instruments the running application so each request can be traced to the code handling it. DAST sees only external behavior, and SAST and SCA do not execute requests.'),
('An unauthorized direct push bypassed required review on a release branch. Which CI/CD control directly addresses the bypass?',
 ['Branch protection with required reviews','A canary deployment for each new release','Nightly regression tests on the release branch','Signing build artifacts after the release'],
 'Branch protection blocks direct pushes and enforces review before merge. Canaries, regression tests and signing act after the unreviewed change is already in.'),
('A reviewed update may interact unexpectedly with real traffic. The team wants to expose only a small controlled population before wider release. Which technique fits?',
 ['A canary to a few percent of users first','A blue-green switch of all traffic at once','A full regression suite in the test environment','A rollback plan stored with the release notes'],
 'A canary exposes a small share of production traffic first and is widened only if it behaves. A blue-green switch moves everyone at once, and test-environment runs do not reproduce real traffic.'),
('A fix changes a shared authorization module. The team must confirm that previously working permission scenarios still behave correctly. Which check answers this?',
 ['Rerun the existing authorization regression tests','Verify the build provenance attestation','Run software composition analysis on the module','Deploy the change to a canary group first'],
 'Regression tests confirm previously working scenarios still pass after the change. Provenance shows origin, SCA checks dependencies, and a canary exposes users to the risk the tests should catch first.'),
('A repository contains an approved source revision, but the deployment team must establish that the received package was produced by the authorized build process. Which check is most relevant?',
 ['Verify a signed provenance attestation for the artifact','Rerun static analysis on the approved revision','Check that the commit has two approving reviewers','Compare the package size with the previous release'],
 'A signed provenance attestation links the artifact to its source and build process. Analysis or review of source does not prove that the delivered package came from that build.'),
('An acquirer needs a structured list of the libraries included in a delivered application so it can identify affected products after a dependency advisory. Which artifact supports this?',
 ['A software bill of materials for each release','A code-signing certificate for the vendor','A vendor attestation of secure development','A penetration test report for the product'],
 'An SBOM lists components and versions so affected products can be found when an advisory appears. Signatures, attestations and test reports do not list the contents.'),
('Before accepting bids, a buyer needs all software suppliers to understand the required authentication, logging and support capabilities. Which item establishes comparable expectations?',
 ['A documented security specification in the tender','An SBOM from each bidder current product','A right-to-audit clause in the final contract','A vulnerability disclosure policy from each bidder'],
 'Written security requirements in the tender give every bidder the same expectations to meet. An SBOM shows what exists, and audit clauses and disclosure policies do not define required capabilities.'),
('A distributor needs recipients to verify that a downloaded release was signed by its authorized publisher and has not changed. Which mechanism provides that evidence?',
 ['A publisher digital signature','A SHA-256 checksum posted on the same download page','A software bill of materials shipped with the release','TLS encryption on the download server'],
 'A signature verified against the publisher\'s trusted public key proves origin and integrity. A checksum on the same page can be replaced along with the file, and TLS protects only the transfer.'),
('Researchers need an established channel and handling process for reporting flaws in a deployed product. Which supplier capability meets this need?',
 ['A vulnerability disclosure policy','A code-signing process for every release','A software bill of materials for each version','An annual third-party penetration test'],
 'A vulnerability disclosure policy tells researchers how to report flaws and how they will be handled. Signing, SBOMs and annual tests do not provide a reporting channel.'),
('A server exposes two unused management services. The approved workload needs neither, and the team wants to remove unnecessary entry points. Which design action addresses the stated goal?',
 ['Uninstall both services the workload does not need','Forward the management service logs to the SIEM','Put the server behind a web application firewall','Require MFA on the two management services'],
 'Removing the unused services eliminates the entry points. Logging, a WAF or MFA leave the services listening and reachable.'),
('A sensitive application depends entirely on one gateway control. Designers want a compromise of that gateway not to remove every protection for the data. Which principle should guide the change?',
 ['Defense in depth with independent layers behind it','Fail-open behavior so the gateway never blocks users','A second identical gateway in an active-passive pair','Least functionality on the gateway operating system'],
 'Independent controls behind the gateway keep protecting the data if it falls. A second identical gateway shares the same weakness, and fail-open removes protection entirely.'),
('An investigation requires correlating identity, gateway and application events, but records exist only on separate local hosts. Which architecture change most directly supports correlation?',
 ['Forward events to a central SIEM','Increase local log retention on each host to a year','Compress the local logs daily to save disk space','Enable verbose debug logging on the application'],
 'Central collection with consistent time lets events from different sources be correlated. Longer local retention or more verbose local logs still leave the records scattered.'),
('A new exfiltration control is deployed. Managers need evidence that it catches the intended transfers while preserving authorized work. Which activity evaluates effectiveness?',
 ['Run realistic tests and measure catch and false alarm rates','Confirm the agent is installed on every endpoint','Review the vendor datasheet and analyst reports for detection claims','Count the policy rules configured in the console'],
 'Testing with representative transfers measures whether the control catches what it should and spares authorized work. Installation, datasheets and rule counts do not show outcomes.'),
('A scheduled reporting job must read one storage location without using an employee\'s password. Which identity design best fits its ongoing nonhuman role?',
 ['A managed workload identity limited to that one bucket','A shared administrator account with a vaulted password','The job owner account with MFA disabled for automation','A break-glass account reserved for scheduled tasks'],
 'A managed workload identity limited to the one location separates automation from people and limits access. Shared admin, a person\'s account or a break-glass account give far more access than needed.'),
('An engineer needs elevated rights for a short approved maintenance window, then should return to ordinary permissions automatically. Which design fits?',
 ['Just-in-time access that lapses automatically','A permanent membership in the administrators group','A separate standing admin account for the engineer','A shared root credential checked out from a vault'],
 'Just-in-time elevation grants rights for the approved window and removes them automatically. Standing admin rights or accounts stay active long after the task.'),
('Employees already authenticate through an enterprise identity provider. A new SaaS application should accept that managed identity lifecycle instead of separate local passwords. Which design fits?',
 ['Federated SSO with the enterprise IdP','Local SaaS accounts synchronized by a nightly script','A password manager shared among the SaaS users','Separate SaaS passwords with a strict rotation policy'],
 'Federation lets the SaaS trust the enterprise identity provider, so joiner and leaver changes apply automatically. Local accounts and passwords create a second lifecycle to manage.'),
('The primary identity provider may be unavailable during recovery. A tightly controlled, audited access path must work independently for essential administrators. Which design addresses this dependency?',
 ['A monitored break-glass account outside it','A just-in-time role issued by the same identity provider','A service principal for each administrator','A secondary federation trust to the same provider'],
 'A break-glass account that does not depend on the identity provider still works when it is down, and it must be protected and monitored. JIT roles and secondary trusts depend on the same provider.'),
('A registration workflow receives a certificate request for a newly acquired company. Before issuance, the operator must establish that the requester is entitled to the claimed identity. Which activity is needed?',
 ['RA proofing of the party making the request','A revocation check against the CA OCSP responder','Signature of the request by the issuing CA','Hostname matching of the requested subject name'],
 'The registration authority proves the requester is entitled to the identity before anything is issued. OCSP and hostname checks validate certificates that already exist.'),
('A certificate request has passed identity validation. Which PKI operation creates the issuer\'s verifiable binding between the subject and its public key?',
 ['The CA signs the certificate with its private key','The RA records the identity proofing evidence','The subject generates a new key pair locally','The relying party checks the OCSP response'],
 'The CA\'s signature over the certificate creates the binding relying parties verify. Proofing and key generation come before it, and OCSP checks happen afterwards.'),
('A certificate chains to a trusted issuer and is within its dates, but the holder\'s private key was reported compromised. Which additional validation addresses this issue?',
 ['Check revocation status through OCSP or a CRL','Confirm the subject alternative name matches','Verify the chain up to the trusted root','Check that the key length meets policy'],
 'A compromised key is handled by revoking the certificate, which relying parties detect through OCSP or a CRL. The name, chain and key length can all still look valid.'),
('A browser receives a valid certificate for an unrelated service from a trusted issuer. Which check should reject its use for the requested website?',
 ['Matching the host against the SAN list','Checking the certificate revocation list for the serial','Verifying the intermediate chain to the root','Checking the certificate validity dates'],
 'The browser must match the requested hostname against the certificate\'s subject alternative names. A trusted chain, valid dates and unrevoked status do not make it valid for another site.'),
('A cloud security requirement calls for blocking prohibited uploads before they reach a SaaS service. The approved architecture routes those uploads through an inspection point. Which control placement fits?',
 ['An inline forward proxy CASB that blocks in transit','An API-mode CASB scanning the tenant afterwards','A CSPM tool checking the SaaS configuration','A SIEM rule alerting on large uploads'],
 'An inline proxy sees the upload in transit and can block it before it arrives. API-mode scanning works on content already stored, and CSPM and SIEM rules do not block.'),
('The security team must inspect existing files already stored in an approved SaaS tenant, including uploads made before the control was installed. Which approach can reach that stored content?',
 ['An API-mode CASB integration','An inline forward proxy on the corporate network','A host firewall rule on each laptop','A cloud workload protection agent'],
 'API integration reads content already in the tenant, including old uploads. An inline proxy sees only traffic that passes through it after installation.'),
('An organization wants to find storage resources whose public-access settings diverge from policy across its cloud accounts. Which assessment targets this condition?',
 ['CSPM configuration checks','Runtime container process monitoring','Inline web proxy inspection of uploads','Endpoint detection on administrator laptops'],
 'CSPM evaluates resource configurations, such as public access, against policy across accounts. Runtime, proxy and endpoint tools observe activity, not storage settings.'),
('A container service needs evidence of unexpected process execution inside running workloads. Which sensor provides the most direct observation?',
 ['A runtime agent such as eBPF on each node','A CSPM scan of the container cluster settings','An image scan in the container registry','An API-mode CASB on the SaaS tenant'],
 'A runtime sensor watches processes as they execute inside workloads. Registry scans and CSPM check images and settings before or apart from execution.'),
('A storage service is correctly encrypted, but its tenant policy permits public reads of confidential objects. Which change directly removes the identified exposure?',
 ['Make the confidential objects private in the bucket ACL','Rotate the storage encryption key to a new version','Enable object versioning on the storage account','Turn on access logging for the storage account'],
 'The exposure is the public read permission, so the policy must change. Encryption at rest decrypts for any authorized request, and versioning or logging leave reads open.'),
('A serverless function needs to read one queue but has broad management permissions over the account. Which change targets the excess authority?',
 ['Cut its role to read access on that queue','Move the function into a private subnet','Encrypt the queue with a customer-managed key','Enable detailed tracing on the function'],
 'Least privilege means the execution role allows only reading the one queue. A private subnet, key choice or tracing do not reduce what the role may do.'),
('A threat model assumes storage administrators can obtain encrypted backups. The application\'s separate data key must remain unavailable to those administrators. Which control boundary is central?',
 ['A KMS key that storage admins cannot use','Bucket policies that hide backups from storage admins','Object lock on the backup storage location','Longer retention for the encrypted backups'],
 'Keeping the data key in a separate key service whose policy excludes storage administrators protects the backups even if they obtain them. Bucket policies are controlled by those same administrators.'),
('An organization needs to investigate who altered a cloud resource\'s policy. Preventive permissions are already in place. Which capability supplies the missing historical evidence?',
 ['Control-plane audit logs such as CloudTrail','A CSPM snapshot of the current policy','The resource tags showing its owner','VPC flow logs for every subnet the resource is attached to'],
 'Control-plane audit logs record who called which management API and when. A current snapshot shows the result, not the actor, and flow logs record network traffic.'),
('Two service replicas run in the same facility and depend on the same power distribution. The requirement is to survive loss of that facility. Which design change addresses the shared failure?',
 ['Deploy replicas in separate availability zones','Add a third replica in the same facility','Put both replicas behind a redundant pair of load balancers','Increase the instance size of each replica'],
 'Surviving facility loss needs replicas in separate failure domains. More or bigger replicas in the same facility, or a load balancer in front of them, still share that power.'),
('A stateless API tier reaches its per-instance processing limit. Additional independent instances can serve any request. Which design meets the increased demand?',
 ['Scale out horizontally behind a load balancer','Scale up each instance to a larger size only','Add a nightly backup of the API servers','Move the tier to a second region in standby'],
 'Stateless instances can be added behind a load balancer to share requests. A standby region or backups add no live capacity, and vertical scaling has a hard ceiling.'),
('A shopping application keeps its only cart state inside one web process. A process replacement loses active carts. Which change best supports replaceable front-end instances?',
 ['Store carts in a shared replicated cache','Enable sticky sessions on the load balancer','Add more front-end instances behind the balancer','Restart the web process less often'],
 'Moving cart state to a shared resilient store lets any instance be replaced without loss. Sticky sessions still lose the cart when that instance dies, and more instances do not share state.'),
('A team has redundant live databases, but an accidental authorized deletion replicated to every copy. Which existing capability must be tested to recover earlier valid data?',
 ['Point-in-time restore from historical backups','Failover to the synchronous replica','Promotion of the read replica to primary','Rebuilding the cluster from the current state'],
 'Replication copied the deletion everywhere, so only a backup from before it can recover the data. Failover and replica promotion bring the same deleted state.'),
('An application collects a sensitive field that no approved feature or retention obligation requires. Which design change removes this unnecessary exposure at its source?',
 ['Stop collecting the field and purge it','Encrypt the field with a separate column key','Label the field as restricted in the catalog','Mask the field in application screens'],
 'Data minimization removes the field at collection and deletes what exists. Encryption, labels and masking still keep a value that has no purpose.'),
('Automated transfer rules cannot distinguish public reports from confidential ones because records have no reliable sensitivity metadata. Which change enables classification-aware enforcement?',
 ['Apply sensitivity labels that DLP rules can read','Encrypt every report with the same shared key','Delete reports older than the retention period','Block all outbound transfers of any report'],
 'Labels give DLP rules the metadata they need to treat public and confidential reports differently. Uniform encryption or a blanket block cannot tell the reports apart.'),
('Developers troubleshoot with full production datasets copied to broadly accessible test environments. The same diagnostics can use masked or synthetic data. Which design boundary should change?',
 ['Swap the copies for fake or scrambled records','Add MFA to the shared test environments','Encrypt the copied datasets at rest','Log developer queries against the copies'],
 'Masked or synthetic data removes the sensitive copies from test entirely. MFA, encryption and logging still leave real production data in broadly accessible systems.'),
('A decommissioning plan requires proof that retained media was sanitized under the approved method. Which lifecycle record supports closure?',
 ['A signed sanitization certificate for each device','The asset inventory entry marked as retired','A purchase order for the replacement hardware','The change ticket approving the decommission'],
 'A sanitization certificate records the method, media and who performed it. Inventory entries, purchase orders and approvals do not prove the media was sanitized.'),
('One developer can change a critical release, approve it and deploy it without another authorized reviewer. Which pipeline change directly introduces independent authorization?',
 ['Enforce a two-person rule on merges','Scope the build runner to a single repository','Pin dependency versions with lock files','Run untrusted jobs on ephemeral runners'],
 'Requiring approval from someone other than the author creates separation of duties. Runner scoping, pinning and isolation are useful but do not add an independent decision.'),
('A build job needs access to one artifact repository but inherits administrative permissions across production. Which change reduces the potential impact of a compromised job?',
 ['Give the job a token scoped to that repository','Require two reviewers on every change to the pipeline definition','Sign the artifacts the job produces','Scan the job dependencies with SCA'],
 'A narrowly scoped, short-lived credential limits what a compromised job can reach. Reviews, signing and scanning do not shrink its production-wide permissions.'),
('A pipeline downloads a dependency using a mutable reference. The approved review must bind to the actual component version and origin used for the build. Which control targets this gap?',
 ['Pin the dependency by version and hash','Run the job on an isolated ephemeral runner','Require approval before the pipeline runs','Scan the final container image for known vulnerabilities'],
 'Pinning by version and hash from a trusted source binds the build to the reviewed component. A mutable tag can change underneath an isolated runner or an approved pipeline.'),
('A CI service executes proposed changes from untrusted contributors. Those jobs must not reach production secrets or privileged release workers. Which boundary is essential?',
 ['Run fork builds on isolated runners with no secrets','Require signed commits from every contributor','Pin all actions in the workflow to commit hashes','Add SAST to the pull request checks'],
 'Untrusted code must run where no production secrets or privileged workers can be reached. Signed commits identify authors but do not make their code safe, and SAST does not isolate execution.'),
('A valid employee session requests a substantially more sensitive function from an unfamiliar device. Policy requires renewed assurance for this context change. Which access action fits?',
 ['Step-up authentication before the sensitive function','Log the request and allow it on the current session','Grant the function and review it the next day','Extend the session lifetime for the new device'],
 'Policy requires renewed assurance, so the user re-authenticates with a stronger factor before proceeding. Logging or reviewing later does not meet the requirement.'),
('A partner needs one reporting API but should not inherit access to all internal applications after authentication. Which authorization choice addresses the boundary?',
 ['An OAuth scope limited to the reporting API','A partner role with access to all internal apps','A VPN connection into the partner network segment','A shared API key used by every partner'],
 'A scoped token grants only the reporting API and its operations. A broad role, a VPN or a shared key would give access beyond the one API.'),
('A worker\'s employment has ended. New logins are blocked, but an existing long-lived session still reaches confidential services. Which action closes the remaining access?',
 ['Revoke the refresh tokens and end active sessions','Disable the account in the HR system only','Wait for the access token to expire naturally','Reset the worker password to a random value'],
 'Revoking tokens and ending sessions cuts off access that already exists. A password reset or an HR change blocks new logins, while the long-lived session keeps working.'),
('A privileged operation is validly authorized and completed. The organization must later establish the actor, target, time and outcome. Which capability addresses that requirement?',
 ['A tamper-protected audit log of privileged actions','Step-up authentication before each operation','A just-in-time role for the operator','A quarterly review of privileged group members'],
 'A protected audit log records who did what, to which target, when and with what result. Step-up, JIT roles and membership reviews control access but do not record actions.'),
('A business specifies that a service should be restored within four hours after disruption. Which metric expresses the planned restoration target?',
 ['Recovery time objective','Recovery point objective','Maximum tolerable downtime','Mean time between failures'],
 'The RTO is the planned time to restore service. The RPO limits data loss, maximum tolerable downtime is the outer business limit, and MTBF measures reliability.'),
('A database recovery plan permits losing no more than fifteen minutes of committed updates. Which metric sets this tolerance for data loss?',
 ['Recovery point objective','Recovery time objective','Mean time to repair','Work recovery time'],
 'The RPO is the maximum acceptable age of recovered data. The RTO and repair times describe how long restoration takes.'),
('Business impact analysis concludes that an outage beyond six hours would cause unacceptable mission harm, even though the restoration target is shorter. Which metric captures this outer boundary?',
 ['Maximum tolerable downtime','Recovery time objective','Recovery point objective','Mean time to detect'],
 'Maximum tolerable downtime is the point beyond which harm becomes unacceptable; the RTO is set inside it. The RPO concerns data, and detection time is unrelated.'),
('During an exercise, service was actually restored two hours after the disruption began. Which value records the observed performance rather than a planned limit?',
 ['The measured recovery time','Recovery time objective in the plan','Maximum tolerable downtime from the BIA','Recovery point objective for the data'],
 'The measured recovery time is what the exercise observed. It is compared with the planned RTO and the BIA limit but does not replace them.'),
]
assert len(ITEMS)==64
